{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199 a period marked by significant political and military challenges. He ascended to the county upon the death of his uncle Raymond II a powerful and influential figure in the Levant. Raymond III faced constant threats from various enemies including the Seljuk Turks the Crusaders of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and neighboring Muslim rulers. His reign was characterized by a determined effort to consolidate power defend Tripoli's borders and maintain its independence.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension to the County*\n\nRaymond III's early life was steeped in the political intrigues of the Crusader states. He was a skilled military leader and diplomat trained in the arts of warfare and negotiation. His claim to the County of Tripoli was not without opposition as several other nobles sought to inherit the title. After Raymond II's death Raymond III skillfully navigated these challenges securing his position through a combination of military prowess and shrewd political maneuvering. He quickly established himself as a strong and capable ruler earning the respect of his vassals and the fear of his enemies.\n\nRaymond III’s reign saw frequent warfare with the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum a major power in the region. The Seljuks frequently launched raids into the County of Tripoli threatening its cities and undermining its authority. Raymond III responded with a series of defensive campaigns often leading his own armies into battle. He also engaged in conflicts with other Crusader states such as the Kingdom of Jerusalem vying for influence and territory in the Levant. His strategic decisions and military leadership were crucial in preserving Tripoli's autonomy amidst these turbulent times.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Diplomacy*\n\nA key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his focus on military 